# config/subsys/Kconfig.console — Console, user shell and login

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# User shell (wave 11)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config USER_SHELL
    bool
    default y if !CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE
    help
      Derived from CONSOLE_PROGRAM (Userspace programs): y when a ring-3
      program is started on the console at boot (by default the native
      shell, userspace/services/sh, /fat/SH.ELF, supervised like the ring-3
      drivers: its topology row says start = true). The in-kernel shell
      becomes a recovery console: it runs only in safe mode, when the volume
      has no console image, when the console program does not take the
      console within SH_START_TIMEOUT_S, or after the supervisor gave up on
      it.

      n (CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE): the kernel shell owns the console, as before
      wave 11.

config SH_START_TIMEOUT_S
    int "User shell: seconds to take the console before the recovery console does"
    range 1 300
    default 10
    help
      After boot, and after each death of the user shell, the kernel
      console waits this long for SH.ELF to claim console input. If it
      has not, the recovery console (the in-kernel shell) takes it.

config CONSOLE_LOGIN
    bool "Console login: the native shell starts only after a secure-attention login"
    depends on !MICRO
    default n
    help
      The console is entered through LOGIND.ELF (userspace/services/logind)
      and the terminal server's secure-attention chord (Ctrl+Alt+Del on a
      keyboard, LOGIN_SAK_SERIAL_BYTE on a serial line). The chord belongs
      to the terminal server, which owns console input: it
      stops serving the shell and asks for the user name and the passphrase
      itself, so no program can draw the prompt or read what is typed. The
      credential (a 32-byte hash of the typed line) is checked by the
      kernel's `login` op of `keys` against the user's `[login.NAME]` row of
      the signed topology: the row lists the public part of the key the
      credential derives on this board (the board's secret is in it, so the
      row alone does not let anyone guess a passphrase offline; no password,
      no hash of one, is stored). The session is a shell in a Domain subtree
      owned by the user (`owner_root`, never 0): its keystore scope.

      Needs the native shell session as the console program (Userspace
      programs: SH.ELF served by TERMD.ELF); with another console program the
      row is there and nothing asks for a login. (Not a `depends on`: the
      console choice depends on the images this one pulls in.)

      y: when the topology has at least one `[login.NAME]` row the kernel does
      not start SH.ELF at boot; LOGIND.ELF starts it after a login. Every
      attempt is a `login` decision record, durable whatever the verdict. With NO
      `[login.NAME]` row nobody is enrolled: the shell starts as it does
      with this off (the component manager starts it) and LOGIND.ELF records
      that the console session is the system's. The terminal server gets its
      call right on the login service only for a console behind a login: there a
      service that does not answer keeps the console locked; without it a
      service that is down cannot take the console.

      n: the shell starts at boot and nothing asks for a credential.

      The rows of `[login.NAME]` are also the SSH server's accounts: a
      credential key listed there logs in on the console, an ssh key logs in
      over SSH, and the row may list both.

config LOGIN_SAK_SERIAL_BYTE
    int "Console login: the byte that is the secure-attention key on a serial console"
    range 0 31
    default 29
    help
      A serial line has no chord. This control byte (29, ^], by default) typed
      on the console is the secure-attention key there: the terminal server
      reads console input, so a program can print what it likes but cannot
      make the terminal type this byte (a terminal's own replies are escape
      sequences). 0: no serial secure-attention key (a keyboard chord only).
      Read by the terminal server; it matters only with CONSOLE_LOGIN.

config RECOVERY_CONSOLE
    bool "Recovery console: a kernel console task for when ring 3 cannot start"
    depends on !CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN
    default y
    help
      crates/core/recovery, kernel/src/tasks/system.rs. The one shell is the
      ring-3 user shell (USER_SHELL); this is the small kernel console that
      takes the UART when that shell cannot (safe mode, no console image on
      the volume, the supervisor gave up, SH_START_TIMEOUT_S passed) or when
      the build has no console program at all (CONSOLE_PROGRAM_NONE). Its
      commands are recovery and diagnosis only: help, mem, uptime, drvls,
      security, estop, ls, cat, crash, ota, config, ifconfig, dhcp, swap, reboot,
      shutdown, and the two bench tools wcet and bench.

      y (default, development builds especially): the kernel creates the
      "recovery" task at boot; it parks until no user shell has the console.
      n (allowed in a release image): the task is not created, boot prints
      "[CONSOLE] no recovery console" and nothing reads the UART once the
      user shell is gone. A board whose ring 3 fails then has no console to
      repair it from, so a release keeps y unless the board is reachable
      another way. CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN forces n and says so itself.

      The crate stays linked either way: it also holds the family bodies the
      ring-3 typed calls run and the ota-recv task entry.

config FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX
    int "Framebuffer console: text columns the grid can hold"
    range 40 512
    default 128
    help
      crates/drivers/fb/src/console.rs. The grid is this many columns by
      FB_CONSOLE_ROWS_MAX rows of one byte each; a screen with more cells
      than that uses the top-left part. 128 columns is 1024 pixels of 8x16
      cells.

config FB_CONSOLE_ROWS_MAX
    int "Framebuffer console: text rows the grid can hold"
    range 20 256
    default 64
    help
      See FB_CONSOLE_COLS_MAX. 64 rows is 1024 pixels of 8x16 cells.

config KERNEL_CMDLINE_INIT
    bool "Honour init=/fat/NAME.ELF on the kernel command line (secure boot off)"
    default y
    help
      The kernel command line (the device tree's /chosen/bootargs; QEMU
      -append) may name the console program for one boot:
      `init=/fat/NAME.ELF`, an 8.3 image directly under /fat whose topology
      row exists and says `start = true` (the component manager starts only
      those, and spawn refuses an image without a row; the row's seccomp
      profile, capabilities and supervision apply). It replaces
      CONSOLE_PROGRAM for that boot. Never with secure boot on
      (SECURE_BOOT_ENFORCED): the word is ignored and the boot log says so,
      since the console program is part of what the signed configuration
      decides. Costs one device-tree walk at boot.

      n: init= is ignored on every build (the log says so).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Console login (the `login` op of the `keys` interface; userspace/services/logind)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# CONSOLE_LOGIN itself is in config/subsys/Kconfig.console (it decides whether the native shell
# starts at boot). These bound the kernel's side of the credential check.

config LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES
    int "Console login: wrong credentials in a row before the account is locked"
    range 1 32
    default 5
    help
      The `login` op of `keys` counts the consecutive refusals of each
      `[login.NAME]` row (and, in one more counter, of every name the topology
      does not list). At this many the account is locked for LOGIN_LOCKOUT_S
      seconds: the op answers DENIED at once, without looking at the
      credential, and writes a `login` decision record (reason 2). A good
      credential before the limit clears the count. The counters are kept in
      RAM, so a reboot clears them.

config LOGIN_LOCKOUT_S
    int "Console login: seconds an account stays locked"
    range 1 3600
    default 30
    help
      How long a locked account refuses every credential. The refusal at the
      lock is the last one counted; the next attempt after the lock lapses
      starts the count again.

config LOGIN_GRANT_TTL_S
    int "Console login: seconds a verified credential may start its session"
    range 1 600
    default 20
    help
      A credential that verifies arms a one-shot grant on the caller's Domain
      (the login service); its next SPAWN_F_USER spawn starts the session and
      spends it. A grant not spent in this many seconds lapses, so a login
      service that stalls cannot sit on an admitted credential.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Console
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config CONSOLE_TX_RING_BYTES
    int "Console UART transmit ring (bytes)"
    range 1024 65536
    default 4096
    help
      Bytes of console output queued for the UART transmit interrupt
      (crates/drivers/sys/src/uart.rs, TX_RING_BYTES). A ring-3
      writer that outruns the wire waits for room; a kernel line that
      does not fit is deferred. 4096 is about 0.36 s of wire at 115200
      baud. Must stay well above the 256-byte line reserve of
      console_defer.rs, or every kernel line is deferred.

      Cost: 1 B of RAM per byte (TX, .bss), the same on riscv64 and aarch64:
      4,136 B at the default.

config CONSOLE_DEFER_BYTES
    int "Console deferred-line buffer (bytes)"
    range 1024 65536
    default 8192
    help
      Kernel console lines written while another context owns the UART
      wait here (crates/drivers/sys/src/uart.rs, DEFER_BYTES). A full
      buffer drops lines and prints "[CONSOLE] dropped", which fails every
      gate row. 8192 is 1.9x the largest high-water mark measured
      (4257 B, aarch64 -smp 2 splice stress, 2026-09-28).

      Cost: 1 B of RAM per byte (DEFER, .bss), the same on riscv64 and aarch64:
      8,240 B at the default.

config CONSOLE_TX_IRQ_BUDGET
    int "Console: most bytes one TX interrupt moves to the UART"
    range 16 4096
    default 256
    help
      crates/drivers/sys/src/uart.rs, TX_IRQ_BUDGET. On a real UART
      the 16-byte FIFO fills first, so this never binds; under QEMU,
      which transmits instantly, it is the bound.
